00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.880
And today is actually
about a blog post.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.700
And it's a really cool blog
post, I really like it.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.130
The title is "How to avoid a
self-inflicted DDoS attack--

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.089
CRE life lessons".

00:01:22.089 --> 00:01:23.130
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, don't.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.010
Like, self-inflicted
harm is just a bad thing.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.426
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The worst part is

00:01:26.426 --> 00:01:28.950
that I've actually done
the things that they say,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.240
do not do this.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.592
I've totally written
code that did this.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.300
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.834
And I think it's one of these
articles where you read it

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:38.250
and you're reading like, oh
yeah that totally makes sense.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.284
But until somebody tells you
to do these things you're like,

00:01:41.284 --> 00:01:42.744
uh why would that be a thing?

00:01:42.744 --> 00:01:43.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.950
So actually, the one
that I wrote was-- it's

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.460
actually the first one because
I don't do that-- where it says,

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.980
try exponential backup
so if something fails.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.670
So what I did was
actually worse--

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.170
MARK MANDEL: Before you go into
that, lets set some context.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.970
So the whole idea
of the blog post--

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.969
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The whole
idea of the blog post

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:03.660
is, it's not best practices.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.960
It's the worst practices.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.750
Like what are the things
that if you write-- like that

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.460
could create issues, right?

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.670
So it's like you
should not just send

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.880
requests on a fixed interval
regardless of the service

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.680
working or not because
then at the end,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.940
basically what you're
doing is you are adding

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.740
more and more and
more services trying

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.480
to connect to your back end.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.510
And the back end is not
up, so when it comes up,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.030
you have a huge amount of
traffic waiting for it.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.210
So instead, you should
try exponential backoff.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.874
What I did was even better.

00:02:34.874 --> 00:02:36.290
I actually wrote
an App Engine app

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.540
that whenever it could fail, it
could send a message to itself.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.960
So yeah, it ended up
being very funny to see

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.920
how it all of a sudden--
App Engine scales very well.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.250
So I was able to send messages
to itself saying I failed.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.179
MARK MANDEL: And then
there were more messages

00:02:55.179 --> 00:02:56.220
that came back to itself.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It was awful.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.140
Software architecture is fun.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.500
So that is one of the things--
try exponential backoff.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.366
It always hurt--
it always helps.

00:03:04.366 --> 00:03:06.990
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I know there
are several really great things

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.250
in here about if
you're building,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.990
particularly, mobile
apps with services

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.290
as a back end that could go
down because software fails.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.180
So techniques like
exponential back off,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.750
adding a little jitter,
implement retry marking, things

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.397
like that-- well worth your
time because it can save you

00:03:21.397 --> 00:03:23.730
a lot of time if something
goes horribly, horribly wrong

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.500
and you need to get
everything back up again.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.416
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:26.416 --> 00:03:28.830
It definitely, definitely
helps avoid outages,

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.320
and especially when
an outage happens,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.690
minimize the effects of it.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.260
So yeah, definitely
check it out.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.240
I think it's a great blog post.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.010
I really enjoyed it.

