known as Alexis MP.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.440
He's going to be telling
us a little bit about who

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.441
are the GDEs.

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:54.690
MARK MANDEL: And what they do.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.106
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So we're here now

00:03:56.106 --> 00:03:59.010
with someone else from our team.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.510
Not a developer advocate though.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.980
But what are you?

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:02.754
Who are you?

00:04:02.754 --> 00:04:04.170
Alexis, could you
tell us a little

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.560
bit real quick who you are
and what do you do at Google.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.140
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: Sure.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.430
I'm Alexis Moussine-Pouchkine.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.950
I usually go by
Alexis MP and I look

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.620
after a number of communities
that we have-- that

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.790
is developer communities.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.850
And one of them is Google
Developer Experts, GDEs,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.404
and specifically, the ones
that are specializing in cloud.

00:04:21.404 --> 00:04:23.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
for the episode today,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.580
we're actually interviewing
a bunch of them.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.430
Could you tell us a little
bit about who are they.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.219
What is a GDE and how
do you become one?

00:04:32.219 --> 00:04:34.760
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: Yes,
that's an excellent question.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.560
So a GDE is somebody that's
passionate, obviously,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.760
about a technology, in our
case Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.880
The program here
is a way for them

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.220
to be actually involved in
speaking to product managers

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.610
and development teams
providing feedback,

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.980
and taking what they
learn and eventually,

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.440
when this hits the
market-- and trying

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.030
to help us get the word out when
it comes to spreading the news

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.180
and speaking publicly,
writing blogs.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.430
So a number of listeners
here will probably

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.460
recognize some of the names
as people authoring blogs

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.275
and may be good public
speakers as well.

00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:15.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
they're all definitely

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.430
very active in
the open community

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:22.180
but I've heard that they also
get privilege access to Google

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:22.790
information.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.540
Like they have an NDA
or something, right?

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.914
ALEXIS MOUSSINE
POUCHKINE: Right.

00:05:25.914 --> 00:05:28.340
And I'll get to the question
about how you become one,

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.350
because that's an
interesting one.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.570
Yes, they're on
the NDA and that is

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:34.940
how we can have some very
interesting conversations

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.290
about the things that is
on the product manager's

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.040
mind-- like the plans
that they have--

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.220
and try to validate those
or amend them, if possible,

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:46.750
and get those people into
early access program,

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.330
maybe in a more
systematic way than it

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:53.330
would be if you were completely
outside that program.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.910
And how do you become one?

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:58.480
Well you really need
to be pretty technical.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.230
We're really looking
for people that

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.610
are able to pretty much be
developer advocates, right?

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.600
We just don't hire them.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.330
We have this relationship
where we share a lot of stuff

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:09.530
under NDA.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:13.280
We bring them to Mountain
View once a year for Summit.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:17.590
That's what's happening this
year and this week, in fact.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:21.100
And we hope that,
in return, we'll

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.850
get feedback from them, and
from the sessions today there

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:26.800
definitely is feedback.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.680
Some of it is really
good and I'm really

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.790
happy about the way it's going.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:37.220
And their part of the job is to
help us get the word out there.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.790
So it's very similar to a
number of other programs.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.420
This one needs to
be very technical

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.120
and we're looking
for influencers

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.600
and we're always looking
for people around the world

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.260
to help us spread the news.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I was
in the room a couple minutes

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.030
before, and I was listening
to one of those talks

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.760
and I got to say they're
a really tough audience.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.512
When a public manager
is like, well we're

00:06:58.512 --> 00:07:00.970
thinking about this and this
and that, rather than be like,

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.920
oh that is nice,
someone could be like,

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.080
what about this other one?

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.340
It's a really tough audience.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:07.700
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: And
that's what we're looking for.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:08.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
They have an opinion.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.488
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
They have an opinion

00:07:10.488 --> 00:07:12.260
and that's why they are GDEs.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.030
We expect the feedback.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.540
We're not looking for
people that will just rehash

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:17.740
whatever the party line is.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:22.130
We're looking for people that
make the messaging, whatever

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.930
it is, theirs and will
amend it and will give it

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:29.576
their full potential, given
what they got out of it.

00:07:29.576 --> 00:07:30.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is awesome.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:31.940
Thank you so much
for taking the time

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.410
to tell us about GDEs today.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:33.910
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
You bet.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.200
MARK MANDEL: Now that we have
a great idea of what GDEs are

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.810
and what they do, why
don't we go talk to some?

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
we're very happy to have

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:40.979
Lynn Langit today.

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:42.020
Hello, how are you doing?

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.436
LYNN LANGIT: Hey,
I'm doing great.

00:07:43.436 --> 00:07:46.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
you're one of the GDEs

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.350
that I know the most because
you write a lot about Windows

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.079
and so many other crazy things.

00:07:53.079 --> 00:07:55.245
Why don't you tell us a
little bit-- what do you do?

00:07:55.245 --> 00:07:56.780
LYNN LANGIT: What do I do?

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.680
I'm an independent cloud
and big data architect.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.100
Most recently I've been working
as lead on an IoT project

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.110
on your competitor's cloud.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.240
But I am actually starting
to do some work now back

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.520
on your cloud in a really
interesting vertical,

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:13.374
and that vertical is
scaling cancer genomics.

00:08:13.374 --> 00:08:15.790
MARK MANDEL: Can you tell us
a little bit more about that?

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:17.090
LYNN LANGIT: Yeah, for sure.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:19.970
Well because I'm independent,
I can pretty much

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.200
pick my projects.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.160
And so, unfortunately, a
friend of mine got cancer.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.450
And I was startled to
see the lack of access

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:31.180
to personalized treatments.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:32.919
And so I started to
do some research,

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:35.960
and I have a teenage daughter
who had the good fortune

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:39.020
to attend Stanford this summer
and study cancer biology.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.690
So she had some
information for me as well.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:45.280
And in looking at the
state of research biology,

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:47.690
I found that there
was an opportunity

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:51.590
to help educate the researchers
about using various vendors'

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:53.390
clouds to scale.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.100
So as I started
to look into this,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.470
I saw that you guys,
here at Google,

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:01.390
actually have a
genomics API in alpha.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:07.120
And being a GDE, I have access
to some of these alpha things

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.050
that you guys have.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:10.630
So I got access to it and
started working with it

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.479
and started learning
biology and here we go.

00:09:13.479 --> 00:09:15.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That sounds awesome.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:15.865
MARK MANDEL: That's
really amazing.

00:09:15.865 --> 00:09:16.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:20.621
So can you share some
of the results of it.

00:09:20.621 --> 00:09:21.620
How was your experience?

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.425
How is it working
with an alpha project?

00:09:24.425 --> 00:09:27.261
LYNN LANGIT: So alpha project--
I mean I build software too,

00:09:27.261 --> 00:09:27.760
right?

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.250
So alpha projects
are alpha projects.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:34.100
But the genomics API really
was very much a minimum

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.620
viable so it was
variant processing.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.470
So you input the data
and then you magically

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:40.460
get out a variant.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.907
So what the heck is a
variant, for those of us

00:09:42.907 --> 00:09:43.990
who don't work in biology?

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.150
So I'm still working
on my understanding

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:49.300
so if you are a biologist
listening, don't cringe,

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:54.070
but the idea is that you've got
genomic sequencing input-- so

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.360
T, A, G, C, all
that kind of stuff--

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.260
and then you compare that
with a reference genome

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.530
and then you find the variations
or the variants, which

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:04.460
I'm coming to understand
is actually a very

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.840
small percentage, less than 1%.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.475
But it's those variants that
are critical in personalized

00:10:10.475 --> 00:10:10.975
medicine.

00:10:10.975 --> 00:10:11.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:12.730
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:15.904
Well, I also know you've been
a GDE for a little while.

00:10:15.904 --> 00:10:17.320
How did you end
up becoming a GDE?

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:18.861
What got you involved
in the program?

00:10:18.861 --> 00:10:21.760
LYNN LANGIT: I did a
presentation on App Engine

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:27.040
