00:11:34.291 --> 00:11:36.570
LYNN LANGIT: It's
called Galaxy 2016.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:40.811
So Galaxy is an emerging tool
out of the biology research

00:11:40.811 --> 00:11:41.310
community.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.200
It was created at Johns Hopkins.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.140
And the idea is
to leverage Docker

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:47.820
so that you can
capture configuration

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:49.569
because another really
interesting problem

00:11:49.569 --> 00:11:51.860
in this space is that
a lot of the research

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:54.330
is not reproducible
because people do it

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:56.820
on their desktop-- they
do it on a VM instance,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.410
they don't capture
all the settings--

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:01.850
and so something as simple and
something that we all know,

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:05.534
using Docker to capture
configuration, can really help.

00:12:05.534 --> 00:12:07.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very,
very interesting.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:08.940
I never thought
that the cloud would

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:12.060
be so helpful to such
interesting and important

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:13.022
things like cancer.

00:12:13.022 --> 00:12:14.730
