00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:40.210
OK, so if I delete
something from Datastore--

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:42.040
so to delete
something you're going

00:37:42.040 --> 00:37:44.530
to use the delete operation
that requires a key--

00:37:44.530 --> 00:37:47.710
and you're going to say
delete this key, right,

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:51.040
is that strongly consistent
or eventually consistent?

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:53.710
MARK MANDEL: So Francesc,
that's a really great question,

00:37:53.710 --> 00:37:56.830
because like are many types of
things in software development,

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:58.420
it depends.

00:37:58.420 --> 00:38:00.910
So basically within
Datastore, if you're

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:03.490
interacting with a record,
whether it's delete or get

00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:05.680
or put, if you're interacting
with it through it's

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:08.764
unique identifying key,
that is strongly consistent.

00:38:08.764 --> 00:38:10.180
So if I put something
in Datastore

00:38:10.180 --> 00:38:11.530
and then ask for
it by its key, I

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:12.580
will get it back straight away.

00:38:12.580 --> 00:38:13.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that
is strongly consistent.

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:15.428
MARK MANDEL: That is
strongly consistent.

00:38:15.428 --> 00:38:17.047
However, generally
speaking, we'll

00:38:17.047 --> 00:38:19.630
go into this in a second, when
you query for stuff-- so you're

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:23.290
looking for, say, like numerous
records, so every person

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:26.050
who has an age over
23, for example,

00:38:26.050 --> 00:38:27.730
that is eventually consistent.

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:28.810
So you have to take
that into account.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:30.726
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
means that, in our case,

00:38:30.726 --> 00:38:34.930
if you have a key, like a
person that is 30 years old,

00:38:34.930 --> 00:38:38.200
and you delete it and then
right after you ask for,

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:41.110
give me all the list of the
people that are over 23,

00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:44.661
you might get that person
back because it's eventually

00:38:44.661 --> 00:38:45.160
consistent.

00:38:45.160 --> 00:38:45.610
MARK MANDEL: Exactly.

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:47.962
And you might not because
it's eventually consistent.

00:38:47.962 --> 00:38:49.420
Now, there are some
things in there

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.044
to make sure it is
strongly consistent.

00:38:51.044 --> 00:38:53.170
There is a thing called
ancestor queries.

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:54.970
So you can tie a
record, essentially,

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:57.700
to another record,
a parent record.

00:38:57.700 --> 00:38:59.410
And when you do that,
what that means is

00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:01.570
when you do a query you, can
then do an ancestor query.

00:39:01.570 --> 00:39:03.760
You're basically saying,
give me every person who's

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:06.970
over 23 who is attached
to this parent record.

00:39:06.970 --> 00:39:08.710
In that case, it
will be consistent.

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:11.150
The trade off there
though is in write speed.

00:39:11.150 --> 00:39:13.600
So when you write
things to Datastore

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:15.790
that are attached
to a single entity,

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:18.690
you can usually do it
around one per second.

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:20.440
So that's a trade off
you have to consider

00:39:20.440 --> 00:39:22.144
when designing how
are you going to use

00:39:22.144 --> 00:39:23.560
and how are you
going to structure

00:39:23.560 --> 00:39:24.820
your data within Datastore.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:25.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

